---
import BlogLayout from "@components/blog-layout.astro";
import Code from "@components/code.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import headshot2025 from "@images/sw-headshot-2025.png";
import headshotRedBg from "@images/sw-headshot-2025-red-bg.png";
---

<BlogLayout
    title="Building Modern Web Applications: A Complete Guide"
    description="Explore the fundamentals of modern web development, from design systems to component architecture"
    date="November 30, 2025"
    author="Shane Wade"
    tags={["web development", "design systems", "typescript", "astro"]}
>
    <p class="text-body">
        In this comprehensive guide, we'll explore the essential principles of
        building modern web applications. From establishing a solid design
        system to implementing scalable component architectures, you'll learn
        the techniques that professional developers use to create maintainable,
        performant applications.
    </p>

    <h2 class="heading-2">The Foundation: Design Systems</h2>
    <p class="text-body">
        A well-crafted design system is the cornerstone of any successful web
        application. It provides <strong>consistency</strong>, improves{" "}
        <strong>development velocity</strong>, and ensures a cohesive user
        experience across your entire product. Think of it as the visual and
        interactive language that your application speaks.
    </p>

    <p class="text-body">
        When building a design system, you should focus on three core pillars:
    </p>

    <ul class="text-body">
        <li>
            <strong>Color palette:</strong> Establish primary, secondary, and semantic
            colors that work harmoniously together
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Typography scale:</strong> Define a consistent hierarchy for
            headings, body text, and supporting elements
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Component library:</strong> Build reusable components that encapsulate
            common patterns and behaviors
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="heading-3">Implementing Utility-First CSS</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        Modern frameworks like <a href="https://tailwindcss.com">Tailwind CSS</a
        >{" "}
        and <a href="https://unocss.dev">UnoCSS</a> have revolutionized how we approach
        styling. Instead of writing custom CSS for every component, we can leverage
        utility classes that map directly to design tokens.
    </p>

    <p class="text-body">
        Here's a simple example of a button using utility classes:
    </p>

    <Code
        code={`<button class="btn-primary">
  Click me
</button>`}
        lang="html"
        wrap
    />

    <p class="text-body">
        This approach reduces the amount of custom CSS you need to write and
        maintains consistency across your application. The <code
            >btn-primary</code
        > class is defined once in your configuration and can be reused everywhere.
    </p>

    <Image
        src={headshotRedBg}
        alt="Professional headshot with red background"
        class="hero-image w-full max-w-md mx-auto"
    />

    <div class="divider my-12"></div>

    <h2 class="heading-2">Component Architecture</h2>
    <p class="text-body">
        Building scalable applications requires thoughtful component
        architecture. Components should be <em>composable</em>, <em>reusable</em
        >, and <em>maintainable</em>. Each component should have a single
        responsibility and a clear interface.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="text-blockquote">
        "The secret to building large apps is never build large apps. Break your
        applications into small pieces. Then, assemble those testable,
        bite-sized pieces into your big application." — Justin Meyer
    </blockquote>

    <h3 class="heading-3">Component Best Practices</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        When designing components, consider these essential principles:
    </p>

    <ol class="text-body">
        <li>
            <strong>Single Responsibility:</strong> Each component should do one
            thing well
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Props Interface:</strong> Define clear, typed interfaces for
            component props
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Composition over Inheritance:</strong> Build complex UIs by composing
            simple components
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Accessibility First:</strong> Ensure all components are keyboard
            navigable and screen reader friendly
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="heading-3">TypeScript Integration</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        TypeScript adds an invaluable layer of type safety to your components.
        Here's how you might define a typed component interface:
    </p>
    <Code
        code={`interface ButtonProps {
  variant: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline';
  size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg';
  disabled?: boolean;
  onClick?: () => void;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}`}
        lang="typescript"
        wrap
    />

    <div class="divider my-12"></div>

    <h2 class="heading-2">Performance Optimization</h2>
    <p class="text-body">
        Performance isn't just about load times—it's about creating a smooth,
        responsive experience that feels instant. Modern web applications should
        be fast by default, not as an afterthought.
    </p>

    <Image
        src={headshot2025}
        alt="Professional headshot 2025"
        class="hero-image w-full max-w-md mx-auto my-8"
    />

    <h3 class="heading-3">Key Performance Metrics</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        Focus on these Core Web Vitals to ensure your application performs well:
    </p>

    <ul class="text-body">
        <li>
            <strong>Largest Contentful Paint (LCP):</strong> Measures loading performance
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>First Input Delay (FID):</strong> Measures interactivity
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS):</strong> Measures visual stability
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="heading-3">Optimization Techniques</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        Here are some practical ways to improve your application's performance:
    </p>

    <ul class="text-body">
        <li>Implement code splitting to reduce initial bundle size</li>
        <li>Use lazy loading for images and non-critical components</li>
        <li>Leverage browser caching with appropriate cache headers</li>
        <li>Optimize images using modern formats like WebP and AVIF</li>
        <li>Minimize JavaScript execution time on the main thread</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="divider my-12"></div>

    <h2 class="heading-2">Testing and Quality Assurance</h2>
    <p class="text-body">
        A robust testing strategy ensures your application remains reliable as
        it grows. Combine different testing approaches to catch issues at
        various levels of your application.
    </p>

    <h3 class="heading-3">Testing Pyramid</h3>
    <p class="text-body">
        Structure your tests following the testing pyramid principle:
    </p>

    <ol class="text-body">
        <li>
            <strong>Unit Tests:</strong> Test individual functions and components
            in isolation
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Integration Tests:</strong> Test how components work together
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>End-to-End Tests:</strong> Test complete user workflows
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="text-body">
        Remember: more tests at the base (unit tests), fewer at the top (E2E
        tests). This provides fast feedback during development while still
        catching critical issues.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="text-blockquote">
        "Testing leads to failure, and failure leads to understanding." — Burt
        Rutan
    </blockquote>

    <h2 class="heading-2">Conclusion</h2>
    <p class="text-body">
        Building modern web applications is a journey that combines technical
        excellence with thoughtful design. By establishing a solid foundation
        with design systems, architecting components thoughtfully, optimizing
        for performance, and maintaining quality through testing, you create
        applications that are not just functional, but delightful to use.
    </p>

    <p class="text-body">
        The web platform continues to evolve, and staying current with best
        practices ensures your applications remain competitive and maintainable
        for years to come. Keep learning, keep building, and most importantly,
        keep shipping! 🚀
    </p>
</BlogLayout>
